%%
%[text] ## Example
%[text] ### Load Data into a Table
%[text] We will [`load`](https://www.mathworks.com/help/releases/R2026a/matlab/ref/load.html) variables `x` and `y` from `RandomLinearData.mat` and then create a [`table` ](https://www.mathworks.com/help/releases/R2026a/matlab/ref/table.html?searchHighlight=table&s_tid=doc_srchtitle)to more easily work with our data.
%[text] ![](text:image:6904) **Try**. [Load](https://www.mathworks.com/help/releases/R2026a/matlab/ref/load.html) data into a table by clicking **Load Data into a Table**. 
% Load Data
load RandomLinearData.mat x y

% Create a table with x and y
Tbl = table(x,y,VariableNames={'x','y'})
  %[control:button:444d]{"position":[1,2]}
%%
%[text] ### Fit Linear Regression Model
%[text] We can use the [`fitlm`](https://www.mathworks.com/help/stats/fitlm.html) command in MATLAB to calculate the coefficients for a regression line. The [`fitlm`](https://www.mathworks.com/help/stats/fitlm.html) command provides us with the model formula, estimated coefficients, and model summary statistics. Here, we will focus only on the coefficients. 
%[text] ![MATLAB icon](text:image:0981) **Tip**. You can remove the semicolon from your `mdl` to view these summary statistics.
%[text] ![](text:image:20b8) **Try**. Use the [`fitlm`](https://www.mathworks.com/help/stats/fitlm.html) function to return the regression model fit to the data stored in `Tbl`, by clicking **Fit Linear Regression Model**.
% Use the fitlm function to return the linear regression model
mdl = fitlm(Tbl); % Remove semicolon to view complete summary of statistics.
  %[control:button:1274]{"position":[1,2]}
% Display linear model coefficients
disp("Model Coefficients:")
disp("Slope (b1): " + mdl.Coefficients.Estimate(2)) % Display b1 obtained from fitlm
disp("Intercept(b0): " + mdl.Coefficients.Estimate(1)) % Display b0 obtained from fitlm
%%
%[text] ### Plot Linear Regression Model
%[text] We can use the [`plot`](https://www.mathworks.com/help/releases/R2026a/matlab/ref/plot.html?searchHighlight=plot&s_tid=doc_srchtitle) function to display the scatter plot with the regression line and the 95% confidence interval. 
%[text] ![](text:image:636a) **Try**.  [Plot](https://www.mathworks.com/help/matlab/ref/plot.html?s_tid=srchtitle_support_results_1_plot) the regression model by clicking **Plot Linear Regression Model**.
% Plot the linear regression model.
plot(mdl)

% Label axes
xlabel("x")
ylabel("y")
hold on % Retain plot on current axes
  %[control:button:43ce]{"position":[1,2]}
%%
%[text] ### Use Linear Regression Model to Predict One Response
%[text] We can use the [`predict`](https://www.mathworks.com/help/releases/R2026a/stats/linearmodel.predict.html) command in MATLAB to predict the response for a single x value. 
%[text] ![MATLAB icon](text:image:4d46) **Tip**. Run the previous section to load the data, create, and plot the linear regression model. 
%[text] ![](text:image:32cb) **Try**. [`Predict`](https://www.mathworks.com/help/releases/R2026a/stats/linearmodel.predict.html) the response, $y${"altText":"y"}, for $x=5.1${"altText":"x=5.1"}, and use [`scatter`](https://www.mathworks.com/help/matlab/ref/scatter.html) to plot the predicted value by clicking **Plot One Predicted Value**. 
% Define the input value for prediction
x = 5.1;

% Use the fitted model to predict y values for the input x
YPred = predict(mdl,x);

scatter(x,YPred,"d","filled",DisplayName="Predicted Value")
hold off % Release all plots from current axes
  %[control:button:6d09]{"position":[1,2]}
%%
%[text] ### Use Linear Regression Model to Predict Multiple Responses
%[text] We can use the [`predict`](https://www.mathworks.com/help/releases/R2026a/stats/linearmodel.predict.html) command for a range of values by specifying a vector. 
%[text] ![](text:image:6765) **Try**. We can [`predict`](https://www.mathworks.com/help/releases/R2026a/stats/linearmodel.predict.html) the responses, $y${"altText":"y"}, for $x=2.4, 3.7, 4.9, \\text{and } 9.2${"altText":"x=2.4, 3.7, 4.9, \text{and } 9.2"} and display them in a [`table`](https://www.mathworks.com/help/releases/R2026a/matlab/ref/table.html?searchHighlight=table&s_tid=doc_srchtitle) by clicking **Predict Values Using the Regression Model**.
% Display the prediction result
xRange = [2.4;3.7;4.9;9.2];

% Use the fitted model to predict V values for the range of x values
VPredRng = predict(mdl,xRange);

% Create a table for the predicted values
PredTbl = table(xRange,VPredRng,VariableNames={'x','PredictedY'});

% Display the predicted values table
disp(PredTbl)
  %[control:button:029f]{"position":[1,2]}
%%
%[text] ## ![](text:image:9341) **Application.** Medical Ventilator Problem
%[text] A positive-pressure ventilator system delivers humidified air to a patient. Over time, water vapor from the humidifier and the patient’s exhalation condenses inside the expiratory tube. The ventilator’s monitoring system records the amount of condensed water every hour, producing the data in [`Ventilator.mat`](file:../../Data/Ventilator.mat) with $\\text{time(s)}${"altText":"\text{time(s)}"} in the variable, t, and $\\text{Volume of Water(mL)}${"altText":"\text{Volume of Water(mL)}"} in the variable, V.
%[text] Fit a linear regression model to the data to estimate the rate of condensation (mL/s). Then, use your model to predict the amount of accumulated condensed water after 7.5 seconds.
%[text] ### Load Data into a Table
%[text] ![](text:image:7530) **Exercise 1.** Load the `Ventilator.mat` data into a table using the [`load`](https://www.mathworks.com/help/releases/R2026a/matlab/ref/load.html) and [`table`](https://www.mathworks.com/help/releases/R2026a/matlab/ref/table.html?searchHighlight=table&s_tid=doc_srchtitle) commands. 
% Load data
load Ventilator.mat t V

% Create a table
VTbl=table(t,V,VariableNames={'t','V'});
%%
%[text] ### Fit Linear Regression Model
%[text] ![](text:image:1586) **Exercise 2.** Find the linear regression model of the dependent variable, $V${"altText":"V"}, to the independent variable, $t${"altText":"t"}, and determine the model coefficients.
% Use the fitlm function to return the linear regression model
Vmdl = fitlm(VTbl);
b1 = Vmdl.Coefficients.Estimate(2)
b0 = Vmdl.Coefficients.Estimate(1)
%%
%[text] ### Plot the Linear Regression Model
%[text] ![](text:image:2df8) **Exercise 3.** Plot the linear regression model.
%[text] ![MATLAB icon](text:image:561b) **Tip**. Use [`hold on`](https://www.mathworks.com/help/matlab/ref/hold.html#d126e768349) to retain the plot on the axes for [Exercise 5](internal:M_082c).
% Plot the linear regression model.
plot(Vmdl)

% Label axes
xlabel("Time (s)")
ylabel("Volume of Water (mL)")

% Add a title
title("Condensate Accumulation in Expiratory Tube")
hold on % Retain current plot when adding new plots
%%
%[text] ### Use Linear Regression Model to Predict One Response
%[text] ![](text:image:33e8) **Exercise 4.** Create predicted responses to the data for $t=${"altText":"t="}7.5 seconds.
% Predict condensate volume at tPred using the fitted model
tPred = 7.5;
Vpred = predict(Vmdl,tPred);
% Show readable message with time and predicted volume
disp("At " + tPred+"s, the condensation will reach a volume of "+ Vpred + "mL.")
%%
%[text] ### Plot the Predicted Response
%[text] %[text:anchor:M_082c] ![](text:image:0e56) **Exercise 5.** Plot the predicted response at $t=${"altText":"t="}7.5 seconds on the regression model plot.
%[text] ![MATLAB icon](text:image:91d4) **Tip**. Use [`hold off`](https://www.mathworks.com/help/releases/R2026a/matlab/ref/hold.html?searchPort=60962#d126e778590) to release all plots from the current axes after labeling the axes and creating a [`legend`](https://www.mathworks.com/help/releases/R2026a/matlab/ref/legend.html).
scatter(tPred,Vpred,"filled","o",SeriesIndex=6) % Add the predicted data point in a new color to the existing graph

% Add a descriptive legend
legend("Data","Predictions","95% Confidence Interval","Prediction at t=7.5 seconds")

% Label axes
xlabel("time(s)")
ylabel("{Volume of water(mL)}")
hold off % Disable hold so that new plot commands clear the current axes and remove existing plots.
%%
%[text] [⇦ Return to the Linear Regression](file:./LinearRegressionSoln.m)
%%
